{"task_id": "Mbpp/305", "solution": "def start_withp(items: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the first two words from a list of strings that both start with 'p' (case-insensitive)\n    from the same original string, and returns them as a tuple. Raises ValueError if no such pair is found.\n    \"\"\"\n    for item_text in items:\n        # Split on whitespace to get tokens, mimicking Parser.splitOnWhitespace.\n        # str.split() naturally handles multiple spaces and discards empty strings.\n        tokens = item_text.split()\n\n        p_words_in_current_item = []\n        for token in tokens:\n            # Check if the token starts with 'p' or 'P', mimicking Word.startsWithP.\n            if token and token[0].lower() == 'p':\n                p_words_in_current_item.append(token)\n                if len(p_words_in_current_item) == 2:\n                    # Found the first pair from the current item, mimicking MatchResult.\n                    return (p_words_in_current_item[0], p_words_in_current_item[1])\n\n    # If the loops complete without returning, no matching pair was found.\n    raise ValueError('no matching pair found')"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "class Array:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the input array and provides safe access to elements and prefix.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, elements: list[int]) -> None:\n        self.elements = elements\n        self.length = len(elements)\n\n    def getPrefix(self, i: int) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns elements from index 0 up to and including i.\"\"\"\n        return self.elements[0 : i + 1]\n\n    def getElement(self, k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Returns the element at index k.\"\"\"\n        return self.elements[k]\nclass Index:\n    \"\"\"Holds and validates index parameters i, k, n.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, i: int, k: int, n: int) -> None:\n        self.i = i\n        self.k = k\n        self.n = n\n\n    def isValid(self) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if the indices satisfy 0 <= i < k < n and n >= 1.\"\"\"\n        return 0 <= self.i < self.k < self.n and self.n >= 1\nclass MaxSumCalculator:\n    \"\"\"Performs validation and computes the maximum sum.\"\"\"\n    def max_sum_increasing_subseq(self, arr: list[int], n: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Computes the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from arr[0..i]\n        ending with arr[k], after validating inputs.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Validate inputs using Index class and array length\n        index_validator = Index(i, k, n)\n        if not index_validator.isValid():\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid indices: n={n}, i={i}, k={k}. Must satisfy 0 <= i < k < n.\")\n        if n != len(arr):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Array length mismatch: n={n} but actual length is {len(arr)}.\")\n\n        array_obj = Array(arr)\n        target_val = array_obj.getElement(k)\n        \n        # dp[j] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[j]\n        # using elements from arr[0...j] (within the prefix arr[0...i])\n        dp = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n        # Fill DP array for the prefix arr[0...i]\n        for j in range(i + 1):\n            dp[j] = arr[j] # The element itself is the minimum increasing subsequence sum ending at j\n            for t in range(j):\n                if arr[t] < arr[j]:\n                    dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[t] + arr[j])\n\n        # Find the maximum sum from dp[0...i] where arr[j] is less than target_val\n        prefix_best_sum = 0\n        found_suitable_prefix = False\n        for j in range(i + 1):\n            if arr[j] < target_val:\n                prefix_best_sum = max(prefix_best_sum, dp[j])\n                found_suitable_prefix = True\n\n        # Calculate the final result including arr[k]\n        if found_suitable_prefix:\n            return prefix_best_sum + target_val\n        else:\n            # If no suitable prefix element is found, the subsequence is just arr[k]\n            return target_val\ndef max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr: list[int], n: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from arr[0..i]\n    and including a given kth element (arr[k]) where k > i.\n    \"\"\"\n    calculator = MaxSumCalculator()\n    return calculator.max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr, n, i, k)"}
