{"task_id": "Mbpp/409", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\nclass TuplePair:\n    \"\"\"Represents a 2-element integer pair and computes its product.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, a: int, b: int):\n        self.a = a\n        self.b = b\n\n    def product(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Computes and returns the product of the two elements.\"\"\"\n        return self.a * self.b\nclass InputList:\n    \"\"\"Container for TuplePair instances and conversion from raw tuples.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, tuples: List[TuplePair]):\n        self.tuples = tuples\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def from_tuples(raw: List[Tuple[int, int]]) -> 'InputList':\n        \"\"\"Converts a list of raw tuples to an InputList of TuplePair objects.\"\"\"\n        return InputList([TuplePair(a, b) for a, b in raw])\ndef tuples_to_input_list(raw: List[Tuple[int, int]]) -> InputList:\n    \"\"\"Convenience wrapper that constructs an InputList from raw tuples.\"\"\"\n    return InputList.from_tuples(raw)\ndef min_product_tuple(tuples: list[tuple[int,int]]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Compute and return the minimum product among the provided 2-element integer tuples.\n    Example usage (must pass SRS acceptance test): assert min_product_tuple([(2, 7), (2, 6), (1, 8), (4, 9)]) == 8\n    \"\"\"\n    input_list_obj = tuples_to_input_list(tuples)\n    products = [tp.product() for tp in input_list_obj.tuples]\n    return min(products)"}
