{"task_id": "Mbpp/462", "solution": "def _build_subset(elements, bitmask):\n    \"\"\"\n    Constructs a subset from elements based on a bitmask, with elements in descending input-index order.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(elements)\n    subset = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):  # Iterate indexes from n-1 down to 0\n        if (bitmask >> i) & 1:  # Check if the i-th bit is set\n            subset.append(elements[i])\n    return subset\ndef combinations_list(elements):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all possible combinations (subsets) of the elements of a given list.\n    Returns combinations ordered by bitmask, with elements inside each combination in descending input-index order.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(elements)\n    if n == 0:\n        return [[]]\n\n    result = []\n    # Iterate through all possible bitmasks from 0 to 2^n - 1\n    for mask in range(0, 1 << n):\n        subset = _build_subset(elements, mask)\n        result.append(subset)\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/463", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef max_subarray_product(arr: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum product of a contiguous subarray in the given array.\n    Handles positive numbers, negative numbers, and zeros effectively.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    # Initialize current_max and current_min to track the maximum and minimum\n    # product ending at the current position.\n    current_max = arr[0]\n    current_min = arr[0]\n\n    # Initialize global_max to store the overall maximum product found so far.\n    global_max = arr[0]\n\n    # Iterate through the array starting from the second element.\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        num = arr[i]\n\n        # Store previous current_max to correctly calculate current_min\n        # before current_max is updated.\n        temp_current_max = current_max\n\n        # The maximum product ending at the current position can be the number itself,\n        # or the number multiplied by the previous max/min product.\n        current_max = max(num, num * temp_current_max, num * current_min)\n\n        # The minimum product ending at the current position can be the number itself,\n        # or the number multiplied by the previous max/min product.\n        current_min = min(num, num * temp_current_max, num * current_min)\n\n        # Update the overall maximum product found.\n        global_max = max(global_max, current_max)\n\n    return global_max"}
